9
00:24:51,428 --> 00:24:57,428
The Scream is an art composition created by
Norwegian artist Edvard Munch in 1893.

10
00:27:37,142 --> 00:27:43,142
The Norwegian name of the piece is Skrik ('Scream'), and the German title under which it
was first exhibited is Der Schrei der Natur ('The Scream of Nature').

11
00:30:22,857 --> 00:30:28,857
The agonized face in the painting has become one of the most iconic images in art, seen as
representing a profound experience of existential dread related to the human condition.

12
00:33:08,571 --> 00:33:14,571
Munch's work, including The Scream, had a formative
influence on the Expressionist movement.

13
00:35:54,285 --> 00:36:00,285
Munch recalled that he had been out for a walk at sunset when suddenly
the setting sun's light turned the clouds "a blood red".

14
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:46,000
He sensed an "infinite scream passing through nature".

15
00:41:25,714 --> 00:41:31,714
Scholars have located the spot along a fjord path overlooking Oslo and have suggested various explanations for the unnaturally orange sky, ranging
from the effects of a volcanic eruption to a psychological reaction by Munch to his sister's commitment at a nearby lunatic asylum.

16
00:44:11,428 --> 00:44:17,428
Munch created two versions in paint and two in pastels, as well as a
lithograph stone from which several prints survive.

17
00:46:57,142 --> 00:47:03,142
Both painted versions have been stolen from public museums, but since recovered.

18
00:49:42,857 --> 00:49:48,857
In 2012, one of the pastel versions commanded the highest nominal price
paid for an artwork at a public auction at that time.

19
00:52:28,571 --> 00:52:34,571
The film explores themes of police, detective, parrot, knife throwing.

20
00:55:14,285 --> 00:55:20,285
The tone is mind_bending and intense.
